death being like a song takes place over  time there's a beginning to it there's a  culmination and then there's an ending I  think it has the possibility of  enhancing our lives rather than taking  something away from them my name is Kate  Munger I'm the founder of the threshold  choir we sing at the bedsides of people  who are dying I started the first choir  in March of 2000 six months later two  more choirs and today there are 150  choirs around the world  in 1990 I spent an afternoon taking care  of a friend of mine who was dying of  hiv/aids  he was comatose and agitated and I  didn't know what to do so I started  instinctively singing to him he started  to calm and I started to calm I knew I  discovered something ancient and that I  wanted to bring back the songs that we  bring to bed sites are mostly songs that  we've written they're short they're some  very very softly and the lyrics offer  comfort they offer serenity they offer  simplicity and ease  we'll knock on the door and try to slip  in to the room where the person is dying  as gently as a butterfly I've come to  sing for you we often see a calming of  the breath sometimes we'll see a toe  tapping or a finger moving a smile and  then when we're done slip out just as  quietly often we'll leave singing many  of the people we've sung for say that  their pain is gone the pain is lessened  that is a miracle to me  you
 

